================================================================================
STRATEGY WEIGHT ALLOCATION METHODS — MATHEMATICAL REFERENCE
================================================================================

1. EQUAL WEIGHT (1/N)
   w_i = 1/N
   REF: DeMiguel et al. (2009) "Optimal Versus Naive Diversification"


2. SHARPE RATIO MAXIMIZATION
   max_w  (w^T μ - r_f) / sqrt(w^T Σ w)
   REF: Sharpe (1966) "Mutual Fund Performance"


3. MARKOWITZ MEAN-VARIANCE
   min_w  w^T Σ w
   s.t.   w^T μ = μ_p
          sum(w) = 1
   REF: Markowitz (1952) "Portfolio Selection"


4. GLOBAL MINIMUM VARIANCE
   min_w  w^T Σ w
   s.t.   sum(w) = 1
   REF: Clarke et al. (2011) "Minimum-Variance Portfolio"


5. RISK PARITY (EQUAL RISK CONTRIBUTION)
   RRC_i = w_i (Σ w)_i / w^T Σ w = 1/N
   REF: Maillard et al. (2010) "Equal Risk Contribution"
        Roncalli (2013) "Introduction to Risk Parity"


6. RISK BUDGETING (GENERALIZED)
   RRC_i = w_i (Σ w)_i / w^T Σ w = b_i,  sum(b_i) = 1
   REF: Bruder & Roncalli (2012) "Risk Budgeting"


7. NAÏVE RISK PARITY (INVERSE VOLATILITY)
   w_i = (1/σ_i) / sum_j (1/σ_j)
   REF: Maillard et al. (2010) "Equal Risk Contribution"


8. KELLY CRITERION
   f* = argmax_f  E[log(1 + f r)]
   For discrete outcomes: f* = (p*b - q)/b
   REF: Kelly (1956) "A New Interpretation of Information Rate"
        Thorp (2006) "The Kelly Criterion"


9. FRACTIONAL KELLY
   f_frac = α * f*,  α ∈ (0,1)
   REF: Thorp (2006) "The Kelly Criterion"


10. HIERARCHICAL RISK PARITY (HRP)
    Step 1: Distance matrix
            d_ij = sqrt(0.5 * (1 - ρ_ij))
    Step 2: Single-linkage clustering
    Step 3: Recursive bisection
            α = 1 - v1/(v1+v2)
            where v = weight variance of cluster
    Step 4: Propagate weights down hierarchy
    REF: López de Prado (2016) "Building Diversified Portfolios"


11. BLACK-LITTERMAN
    μ_BL = [(τΣ)^-1 + P^T Ω^-1 P]^-1 [(τΣ)^-1 Π + P^T Ω^-1 Q]
    REF: Black & Litterman (1992) "Global Portfolio Optimization"


12. REGIME-CONDITIONED DYNAMIC ALLOCATION
    w_t = f(regime_t, performance_history_t)
    Methods:
    a) HMM-based switching: w_t = w_regime_k
    b) Rolling window with regime filter: w_t = f(returns_t-window:t)
    REF: Ang & Bekaert (2002) "Regime Switches"


13. BAYESIAN DYNAMIC WEIGHTING
    p(w_t | D_t) ∝ p(D_t | w_t) p(w_{t-1})
    REF: Avramov (2002) "Bayesian Portfolio Optimization"


14. DIVERSIFICATION RATIO MAXIMIZATION
    max_w  (w^T σ) / sqrt(w^T Σ w)
    REF: Choueifaty & Coignard (2008) "Diversification Ratio"


15. CONSTRAINED OPTIMIZATION WITH:
    - No short-selling: w_i ≥ 0
    - Long-only: w_i ≥ 0
    - Sector caps: sum(w_i ∈ sector_k) ≤ cap_k
    - Max individual weight: w_i ≤ max_cap
    - Sum to one: sum(w) = 1


16. DYNAMIC WEIGHT UPDATE (ROLLING PERFORMANCE)
    - Each period: w_i = f(performance_i, window)
    - Options: 
      a) Rolling Sharpe: w_i = Sharpe_i(t-window:t) / sum(Sharpe)
      b) Rolling Return: w_i = Return_i(t-window:t) / sum(Return)
      c) Rolling Sortino: w_i = Sortino_i(t-window:t) / sum(Sortino)
    REF: Kirby & Ostdiek (2012) "It's All in the Timing"


17. CORRELATION-PENALIZED WEIGHTING
    Pairwise penalty: λ * ρ_ij * w_i * w_j
    Objective: max_w  (w^T μ - λ * w^T C_penalty w)
    REF: DeMiguel et al. (2009) "Optimal Versus Naive Diversification"


18. ENSEMBLE STRATEGY WEIGHTING (MACHINE LEARNING)
    - Simple average: w_i = 1/N
    - Weighted average by performance
    - Stacking: meta-model learns optimal weights
    - Bayesian Model Averaging: w_i = P(model_i | data)
    REF: Wolpert (1992) "Stacked Generalization"

================================================================================
NOTATION REFERENCE
================================================================================

Symbol    Definition
------    ----------
w         Weight vector (w₁, w₂, ..., w_N), sum(w) = 1
μ         Expected return vector
Σ         Covariance matrix
σ_i       Volatility of strategy i
ρ_ij      Correlation between strategy i and j
r_f       Risk-free rate
f*        Kelly-optimal fraction
τ         Black-Litterman uncertainty scaling
P         Pick matrix (views)
Q         View returns vector
Ω         Uncertainty matrix of views
Π         Equilibrium risk premiums
RRC_i     Risk contribution of strategy i
b_i       Target risk budget of strategy i
D_t       Data available up to time t
v         Weight variance of cluster

